  "result": "Comparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6731view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view5\",\"lineno\":121}); Total lines: 573\nL111: HuggingFace TGI provides a production-ready serving solution with features including model parallelism, quantization, speculative decoding, and extensive model support. TGI emphasizes ease of deployment and integration with the HuggingFace ecosystem.\nL112: \nL113: TensorRT-LLM (NVIDIA) offers highly optimized inference through kernel fusion, INT8/FP8 quantization, and multi-GPU parallelism, but requires model-specific optimization and compilation.\nL114: DeepSpeed-FastGen focuses on dynamic splitting techniques to improve throughput for variable-length generation workloads.\nL115: \nL116: Other systems include FasterTransformer, Triton Inference Server, and Ray Serve, each with different design philosophies and optimization strategies.\nL117: ### 2.3 Benchmarking Studies\nL118: \nL119: While several benchmarking efforts exist, they often have limited scope. Prior work has examined individual aspects such as memory efficiency or single-model performance, but comprehensive comparisons across multiple dimensions remain scarce. Our work extends previous efforts by providing systematic evaluation across model sizes, workload patterns, and performance metrics relevant to production deployment decisions.\nL120: \nL121: ## 3 Methodology\nL122: \nL123: ### 3.1 Experimental Setup\nL124: #### 3.1.1 Hardware Configuration\nL125: \nL126: All experiments were conducted on NVIDIA A100 GPUs (80GB) to ensure sufficient memory for large models. We used the following infrastructure:\nL127: \nL128:   * •\nL129: \nL130: GPU: 4x NVIDIA A100 80GB (SXM4)\nL131: \nL132:   * •\nL133: \nL134: CPU: AMD EPYC 7763 64-Core Processor\nL135: \nL136:   * •\nL137: \nL138: RAM: 512GB DDR4\nL139: \nL140:   * •\nL141: \nL142: Storage: NVMe SSD for model loading\nL143: \nL144:   * •\nL145: \nL146: Network: 100Gbps interconnect\nL147: #### 3.1.2 Software Versions\nL148: \nL149:   * •\nL150: \nL151: vLLM v0.6.1\nL152: \nL153:   * •\nL154: \nL155: HuggingFace TGI v2.3.0\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: \nL159: CUDA 12.1\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: \nL163: PyTorch 2.1.0\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: \nL167: Model: LLaMA-2-7B, 13B, and 70B\nL168: ### 3.2 Models and Configurations\nL169: \nL170: We evaluated three LLaMA-2 model variants:\nL171: \nL172:   1. 1.\nL173: \nL174: LLaMA-2-7B: 7 billion parameters, single GPU deployment\nL175: \nL176:   2. 2.\nL177: \nL178: LLaMA-2-13B: 13 billion parameters, single GPU deployment\nL179: \nL180:   3. 3.\nL181: \nL182: LLaMA-2-70B: 70 billion parameters, tensor parallel across 4 GPUs\nL183: \nL184: For the 70B model, we used tensor parallelism to distribute the model across multiple GPUs. Both frameworks were configured with FP16 precision to balance performance and quality.\nL185: ### 3.3 Workload Characteristics\nL186: \nL187: We designed benchmark workloads to reflect realistic deployment scenarios:\nL188: \nL189: #### 3.3.1 Request Patterns\nL190: \nL191:   * •\nL192: \nL193: Low Concurrency (1-10 users): Simulates interactive chatbot scenarios\nL194: \nL195:   * •\nL196: \nL197: Medium Concurrency (10-50 users): Typical production load\nL198: \nL199:   * •\nL200: \nL201: High Concurrency (50-200 users): Stress test for peak demand\nL202: #### 3.3.2 Input/Output Characteristics\nL203: \nL204:   * •\nL205: \nL206: Prompt lengths: Sampled from Poisson distribution (mean=512 tokens)\nL207: \nL208:   * •\nL209: \nL210: Generation lengths: Sampled from Poisson distribution (mean=256 tokens)\nL211: \nL212:   * •\nL213: \nL214: Temperature: 0.7 with top-p sampling (p=0.9)\nL215: \nL216: #### 3.3.3 Dataset\nL217: \nL218: We used prompts from the ShareGPT dataset, which contains real user-AI conversations, ensuring realistic input distributions. Each benchmark run consisted of 1,000 requests to ensure statistical significance.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6731view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view6\",\"lineno\":83}); Total lines: 276\nL76: where $\\tau_{s}$ is a semantic similarity threshold. The challenge is that contamination at level 3 (semantic) and level 4 (reasoning pattern) evades existing detection methods despite inflating evaluation metrics.\nL77: ### 3.2 Hierarchical Detection Framework\nL78: \nL79: Our framework processes synthetic training data through four hierarchical levels, with each level designed to catch contamination types missed by previous levels.\nL80: Level 1: Token Level Detection We employ Min-K% Prob (cite41†Deng et al. 2024b ) to identify pretraining data without access to the training corpus. For each test sample $x_{j}^{b}$, we compute token-level logarithmic probabilities $\\{\\log p(t_{k}|t_{<k})\\}_{k=1}^{|x_{j}^{b}|}$ from the target model. The Min-K% score aggregates the lowest K% of probabilities:\nL81: \nL82:  | $$\\text{Score}_{\\text{MinK}}(x_{j}^{b})=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k\\in\\text{bottom-K\\%}}\\log p(t_{k}|t_{<k})$$  |  | (2)\nL83: Samples with scores above threshold $\\tau_{1}$ are flagged as token-level contaminated. This baseline effectively detects verbatim inclusion and near-duplicates.\nL84: \nL85: Level 2: Semantic-Level Detection. To detect paraphrased contamination, we combine three complementary signals. First, we compute embedding similarity using sentence transformers (cite47†Reimers and Gurevych 2019 ):\nL86:  | $$\\text{sim}_{\\text{emb}}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})=\\frac{\\mathbf{h}(x_{i}^{s})^{\\top}\\mathbf{h}(x_{j}^{b})}{||\\mathbf{h}(x_{i}^{s})||\\cdot||\\mathbf{h}(x_{j}^{b})||}$$  |  | (3)\nL87: Second, we perform embedding space clustering using DBSCAN to identify synthetic samples that cluster near benchmark embeddings in the representation space. Third, we analyze distributional characteristics: benchmark clusters should exhibit distinct geometric properties (higher density, tighter variance) compared to typical training data. Samples satisfying $\\text{sim}_{\\text{emb}}>\\tau_{2}$ AND cluster membership AND distributional anomaly detection are flagged.\nL88: Level 3: Reasoning Pattern Detection. For reasoning tasks, we extract chain-of-thought traces from model outputs and compute reasoning pattern similarity using structured comparison.\nL89: \nL90:  | $$\\text{sim}_{\\text{reasoning}}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})=\\alpha\\cdot\\text{StructSim}+\\beta\\cdot\\text{StepSim}+\\gamma\\cdot\\text{ArgSim}$$  |  | (4)\nL91: where StructSim measures logical structure similarity, StepSim compares reasoning steps, and ArgSim evaluates argument patterns. Samples exhibiting suspiciously similar reasoning chains to benchmark exemplars beyond what would be expected from common problem-solving strategies are flagged.\nL92: Level 4: Performance-Cliff Detection. We leverage model behavior on benchmark variants. If a model performs significantly better on original benchmarks versus semantically equivalent paraphrased versions, this suggests memorization rather than understanding. For each benchmark, we generate K paraphrased variants and compute:\nL93: \nL94:  | $$\\Delta_{\\text{cliff}}=\\text{Acc}(B_{\\text{orig}})-\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\text{Acc}(B_{\\text{para}}^{(k)})$$  |  | (5)\nL95: Statistical significance testing (paired t-test) determines whether $\\Delta_{\\text{cliff}}$ exceeds natural variation, providing an aggregate contamination signal.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6731view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view6\",\"lineno\":110}); Total lines: 276\nL104: We construct four controlled contamination scenarios with increasing subtlety: (S1) Token-Level: synthetic data contains exact benchmark questions, (S2) Paraphrase: GPT-4 generated semantic paraphrases of benchmark questions preserving meaning, (S3) Semantic: synthetic data clusters conceptually near benchmarks (e.g., similar math problem types, related programming tasks) without direct paraphrase, generated using topic-guided synthesis, (S4) Reasoning-Pattern: models generate synthetic data exhibiting similar reasoning chains to benchmark exemplars through few-shot prompting with benchmark-style reasoning patterns.\nL105: Ground truth contamination labels determined through expert annotation by three ML researchers specializing in LLM evaluation.\nL106: Baseline Methods. We compare against: (1) N-gram Overlap (13-gram matching following GPT-3 (cite32†Brown et al. 2020 )), (2) Min-K% Prob (cite41†Deng et al. 2024b ), (3) Embedding Similarity (cosine similarity with sentence-transformers), (4) LLM Decontaminator (cite36†Yang et al. 2023 ) combining embedding search with GPT-4 semantic judgment, (5) CDD (cite52†Dong et al. 2024 ) using output distribution peakedness.\nL107: Evaluation Metrics. We report precision, recall, and F1 score for contamination detection. Statistical significance assessed using McNemar’s test with $p<0.05$.\nL108: ### 4.2 Results\nL109: \nL110: Table cite53†1 presents detection performance across contamination scenarios. On token-level contamination (S1), all methods perform well, with our hierarchical framework achieving F1=0.96 comparable to Min-K% Prob (0.945) and marginally better than n-gram overlap (0.925). This validates that our framework does not sacrifice performance on easier detection tasks.\nL111: Table 1: Contamination detection performance across scenarios and benchmarks (F1 scores). Best results in bold, second-best underlined.\nL112: Method  | S1  | S2  | S3  | S4  | Avg\nL113: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL114: N-gram  | .925  | .400  | .165  | .210  | .425\nL115: Min-K%  | .945  | .610  | .230  | .250  | .509\nL116: Embed Sim  | .520  | .695  | .490  | –  | .568\nL117: LLM Decon  | .780  | .810  | .550  | –  | .713\nL118: CDD  | .890  | .740  | .470  | .310  | .603\nL119: Ours  | .960  | .875  | .755  | .650  | .810\nL120: Improv.  | +.015  | +.065  | +.205  | +.340  | +.097\nL121: Performance diverges sharply on paraphrase contamination (S2). N-gram overlap fails catastrophically (F1=0.40) as expected, while embedding similarity (0.695) and LLM Decontaminator (0.81) show partial effectiveness. Our hierarchical framework achieves F1=0.875, a 6.5% improvement over the best baseline, demonstrating that combining multiple semantic signals enhances detection robustness.\nL122: The critical gap emerges on semantic-level contamination (S3) where synthetic data clusters conceptually near benchmarks without lexical or paraphrase overlap. N-gram overlap achieves only F1=0.165, detecting fewer than one in six contaminated samples. Embedding similarity improves to 0.49 but suffers from threshold selection challenges and high false positive rates. LLM Decontaminator reaches 0.55 but remains limited.\nL123: Our hierarchical framework achieves F1=0.755, representing a 20.5% absolute improvement and demonstrating the effectiveness of embedding clustering combined with distributional analysis.\nL124: On reasoning-pattern contamination (S4), existing methods provide virtually no detection capability (F1=0.21-0.31) as this contamination type has not been previously addressed. Our framework’s CoT trace analysis provides novel detection capability with F1=0.65, though with room for improvement given the complexity of reasoning pattern comparison.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFT-NCFM: An Influence-Aware Data Distillation Framework for Efficient VLA Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16233v1)\nciteturn6731view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view1\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 523\nL116: #### Stage 2: Contrastive Verification Refinement\nL117: \nL118: To further ensure that samples with a high $Score_{base}$ are indeed positively valued and contribute to generalization, we perform contrastive verification on the top-K% elite samples.\nL119: For each elite sample $d_{i}$, we generate a ”minimal counterexample” $d_{\\text{contrast}}$ using a Cross-Modal Mismatch strategy common in robot robustness evaluation. Specifically, while keeping the language ($L$) and action ($A$) sequences fixed, we programmatically modify the initial visual scene ($V$) in a simulator to create a new scene ($V^{\\prime}$) with semantic or physical contradictions, as illustrated in Figure cite73†2 .\nL120: Programmatic Counterexample Generation. This strategy’s automation and generalizability are rooted in the structured VLA task space. Since task categories are limited and classifiable (e.g.,  40 in the LIBERO dataset), we can pre-design a small set of Reusable Perturbation Templates. As detailed in Figure cite73†2 , for any given elite sample $d_{i}$, our script generates a counterexample $d_{\\text{contrast}}$ through a fully automated ”Template Matching and Instantiation” process.\nL121: This process, which ensures efficiency and consistency, involves three stages: (1) Instruction Semantic Parsing, (2) Perturbation Template Selection, and (3) Scene Instantiation via simulator APIs to create a semantically contradictory scene. The effects of our core templates (e.g., object substitution, changing spatial relations) are detailed in Figure cite74†5 .\nL122: After obtaining the counterexample, our goal is to quickly evaluate whether $d_{i}$ is more helpful than $d_{contrast}$ for a relevant standard test case $d_{test}$, without retraining the model. To this end, we use gradient dot products to approximate influence, calculating the influence scores of the elite sample and its counterexample on the test case $d_{test}$ respectively:\nL123:  | $\\displaystyle Score_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\nabla_{\\theta}L(\\Phi(d_{test}),\\hat{\\theta})^{T}\\cdot\\nabla_{\\theta}L(\\Phi(d_{i}),\\hat{\\theta})$  |  | (3)\nL124:  | $\\displaystyle Score_{contrast}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\nabla_{\\theta}L(\\Phi(d_{test}),\\hat{\\theta})^{T}\\cdot\\nabla_{\\theta}L(\\Phi(d_{contrast}),\\hat{\\theta})$  |  | (4)\nL125: An ideal elite sample $d_{i}$ should exhibit high positive influence, while its counterexample $d_{contrast}$ should show negative or significantly lower influence. To convert this contrastive verification result from a discrete binary decision (pass/fail) into a continuous weight modulation mechanism, we designed the following formula for the final influence weight $w_{i}$:\nL126:  | $\\displaystyle w_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=Score_{base}(d_{i})\\times$  |\nL127:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\quad\\left(1+\\tanh\\left(\\beta\\cdot(Score_{i}-Score_{contrast})\\right)\\right)$  |  | (5)\nL128: In this equation, the hyperparameter $\\beta$ controls the sensitivity of the modulation function. The core difference term $(Score_{i}-Score_{contrast})$ quantifies the relative effectiveness of the original sample $d_{i}$ compared to its counterexample. We use the hyperbolic tangent function (tanh) to smoothly map this difference to the $[-1,1]$ interval, thereby constructing a continuous Weight Modulation Factor ‘(1 + tanh(…))‘ ranging from $[0,2]$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAeroVerse: UAV-Agent Benchmark Suite for Simulating, Pre-training, Finetuning, and Evaluating Aerospace Embodied World Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2408.15511v2)\nciteturn6731view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view2\",\"lineno\":322}); Total lines: 580\nL319: This stage reinforces the model’s spatial modeling and causal reasoning capabilities through complex questions in the SkyAgent-Reason3k dataset, laying the groundwork for subsequent navigation tasks. The Aerospace Embodied Navigational Exploration task requires the drone to explore the environment based on long-distance navigation instructions (e.g., “Fly forward 200 meters and then turn left”) and answer attribute-related questions about objects encountered along the way.\nL320: The navigation task introduces a temporal dimension and action sequences, requiring the model to translate the perception and reasoning abilities learned in the previous stages into concrete actions. Through training on the SkyAgent-Nav3k dataset, the model learns to achieve a preliminary “perception-reasoning-action” loop in complex urban environments.\nL321: The Aerospace Embodied Task Planning task is positioned as the advanced planning stage in the curriculum. This task requires the drone to generate detailed path plans (e.g., “First go straight to the red building, then turn right and bypass the tall building”) based on the starting and ending points. This stage is an extension of the navigation task but places greater emphasis on global planning capabilities.\nL322: ### VII-C SkyAgent & SkyAgentX\nL323: As illustrated in Figure cite96†12 , based on a pre-trained general vision-language model Internvl-2.5-8B[cite97†39 ], we introduce the aerospace embodied chain-of-thought mechanism and fine-tune it using specialized instruction datasets (i.e., SkyAgent-Scene3k, SkyAgent-Reason3k, SkyAgent-Nav3k, and SkyAgent-Plan3k) for four downstream tasks, to develop the SkyAgent model.\nL324: On this foundation, we further incorporate the aerospace embodied multitask curriculum learning strategy, progressively training the model through multi-task joint training in the sequential order of scene perception, spatial reasoning, navigation exploration, and task planning. This process ultimately led to the construction of the UAV-agent embodied large model, SkyAgentX, which integrates “perception-reasoning-navigating-planning” into an unified framework.\nL325: ## VIII Results\nL326: ### VIII-A Quantitative Analysis\nL327: TABLE I: Evaluation results on the shanghai city test dataset:SkyAgent-Scene3k, SCENE refers to LLM-JUDGE-SCENE.\nL328: City$\\rightarrow$  | ShangHai  | ShenZhen  | Campus  | Residence\nL329: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL330: Models$\\downarrow$  | BLEU  | SPICE  | SCENE  | BLEU  | SPICE  | SCENE  | BLEU  | SPICE  | SCENE  | BLEU  | SPICE  | SCENE\nL331: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL332: 3d-llm[cite44†10 ]  | 0.0346  | 0.0162  | 0.1660  | 0.0283  | 0.0117  | 0.1156  | 0.0429  | 0.0197  | 0.1511  | 0.0335  | 0.0105  | 0.1378\nL333: GPT-4-vision-preview[cite57†21 ]  | 0.1200  | 0.0884  | 0.6840  | 0.1039  | 0.0924  | 0.6909  | 0.1035  | 0.0901  | 0.6511  | 0.1246  | 0.0999  | 0.7444\nL334: GPT-4o[cite57†21 ]  | 0.1539  | 0.1114  | 0.6800  | 0.1532  | 0.1277  | 0.7178  | 0.1237  | 0.1175  | 0.6977  | 0.1606  | 0.1225  | 0.7089\nL335: blip2-flan-t5-xxl[cite90†36 ]  | 0.1954  | 0.0860  | 0.4041  | 0.1932  | 0.0956  | 0.4333  | 0.2151  | 0.0906  | 0.4318  | 0.2593  | 0.1307  | 0.5089\nL336: blip2-opt-6.7b[cite90†36 ]  | 0.1968  | 0.0814  | 0.4201  | 0.2279  | 0.0836  | 0.4289  | 0.2140  | 0.0906  | 0.4533  | 0.2558  | 0.1102  | 0.4778\nL337: instructblip-flan-t5-xxl[cite98†40 ]  | 0.2118  | 0.0808  | 0.4908  | 0.1972  | 0.0852  | 0.4689  | 0.2149  | 0.0969  | 0.5067  | 0.2536  | 0.1202  | 0.5400\nL338: instructblip-vicuna-7b[cite98†40 ]  | 0.2239  | 0.0787  | 0.4911  | 0.2102  | 0.0835  | 0.5022  | 0.2109  | 0.0867  | 0.4889  | 0.2729  | 0.1193  | 0.5511\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6731view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6727view3\",\"lineno\":233}); Total lines: 541\nL225: Next, we note that this dataset is limited in its range of protected groups for each attribute; for instance, gender is labeled as binary, and ethnicity is labeled according to three groups (Caucasian, Asian, and Black). If used in real-world applications, this can reinforce rigid societal norms and harm individuals who exist outside of these groups.\nL226: Lastly, the age attribute has only been labeled for individuals in the Caucasian ethnicity group; therefore, when using this label in any analysis, we are limited to a smaller amount of data and cannot observe intersections with other protected groups.\nL227: ## V Results and Discussion\nL228: \nL229: In this section, we present the results of our three experiments. Due to ethical considerations outlined in Section cite21†IV , we do not release any pretrained models. Our experiments evaluated multiple model variants to assess predictive accuracy, measure potential bias, and analyze the impact of counterfactual attribute manipulation on model fairness.\nL230: To assess the effect of different modalities and input types, we evaluated the following variants of our multi-task deep neural network (MTDNN) model:\nL231: \nL232:   * •\nL233: \nL234: MTDNN: The full model using all modalities, including visual, audio, and text transcript features.\nL235: \nL236:   * •\nL237: \nL238: MTDNN-Visual: Model using only visual features extracted from video frames.\nL239: \nL240:   * •\nL241: \nL242: MTDNN-Audio: Model using only audio features from the video’s soundtrack.\nL243: \nL244:   * •\nL245: MTDNN-Text: Model using only text-based features derived from video transcripts.\nL246: \nL247: We also considered reduced-frame and GAN-based variations of these models:\nL248: \nL249:   * •\nL250: \nL251: Reduced-frame models:\nL252: \nL253:     * –\nL254: \nL255: MTDNN-10F: Full model using only the first 10 frames from each video.\nL256: \nL257:     * –\nL258: \nL259: MTDNN-Visual-10F: Visual-only model using the first 10 frames.\nL260: \nL261:   * •\nL262: \nL263: GAN-inverted models (using visual features derived from inversion to GAN latent space, without attribute manipulation):\nL264: \nL265:     * –\nL266: MTDNN-Inv: Full model using GAN-inverted visual features. These models serve as a reference to isolate the effects of GAN inversion (e.g., visual artifacts) from those of protected attribute modification.\nL267: \nL268:     * –\nL269: \nL270: MTDNN-Visual-Inv: Visual-only version of the above.\nL271: \nL272:   * •\nL273: \nL274: Counterfactually modified models (based on latent space editing to simulate different protected attributes):\nL275: \nL276:     * –\nL277: \nL278: MTDNN-Inv-Mod: Full model using counterfactually modified video frames.\nL279: \nL280:     * –\nL281: MTDNN-Visual-Inv-Mod: Visual-only model using counterfactually modified video frames.\nL282: \nL283: In addition, for Experiment 3, we introduced a separate multi-task classification model:\nL284: \nL285:   * •\nL286: \nL287: P-Attribute: A model trained to predict perceived protected attributes (gender, ethnicity, and age) from video frames. This model was used to evaluate the effectiveness of our counterfactual video generation process.\nL288: This comprehensive set of models enabled us to analyze predictive accuracy, identify biases across demographic groups, and assess how counterfactual modifications influence fairness metrics.\nL289: cite106†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 3: Distribution of OCEANI dimension scores in the original dataset ground-truth labels, grouped by gender, ethnicity, and age. Each plot shows the distribution for all individuals, the protected group (“M” for male, “Af.Am.” for African-American individuals, and “$\\text{A}\\geq 40$” for individuals aged 40 and above), and the unprotected group (“F” for female, “Asi.+Cau.” for Asian and Caucasian individuals, and “$\\text{A}<40$” for individuals under 40).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTensor Gauge Flow Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17616v1)\nciteturn6731view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17616v1\",\"lineno\":175}); Total lines: 466\nL155: $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is the Tensor Gauge Field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: \nL159: $d^{\\mu}(x(t),t)\\in TM$ is the direction vector field.\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: \nL163: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$, which can be parameterized by a Neural Network.\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: \nL167: $\\Pi_{M}:C\\rightarrow TM$ is a smooth bundle map projection from the vector bundle $C$ to the tangent bundle $TM$ of the base manifold $M$.\nL168: The Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is encoded by the Fiber Bundle $B$. The fibers of $B$ are different for the following two cases:\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: \nL172: $L_{\\infty}$-algebra-valued case: The fibers of $B$ carry a graded vector space $\\hat{B}$ underlying an L_{∞}-algebra, and $\\mathcal{A}$ is a smooth section of the tensor bundle $T^{(m,n)}M$ with values in $\\hat{B}$:\nL173:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\mathcal{A}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{B},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{B}}\\circ\\mathcal{A}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL174: \nL175:   * •\nL176: \nL177: Lie-algebra-valued case: The fibers of $B$ carry a Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$:\nL178: \nL179:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\mathcal{A}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\mathfrak{g},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\mathfrak{g}}\\circ\\mathcal{A}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL180: The corresponding Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$ with typical fiber $P$, such that:\nL181: \nL182:   * •\nL183: \nL184: $L_{\\infty}$-algebra-valued case: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ takes values in a graded fiber $\\hat{P}$, which is equipped fibrewise with the same L_{∞}-algebra structure as $\\hat{B}$:\nL185: \nL186:  | $$\\hat{T}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\hat{T}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{P},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{P}}\\circ\\hat{T}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL187: \nL188:   * •\nL189: Lie-algebra representation case: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ takes values in a representation $P$ of $\\mathfrak{g}$:\nL190: \nL191:  | $$\\hat{T}(x(t),t\\in\\{\\hat{T}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes P,\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes P}\\circ\\hat{T}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL192: \nL193: The action of the Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}$ on the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}$ differs in the two cases:\nL194: \nL195:   * •\nL196: \nL197: Action of a Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$ on the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\nL198:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]:=\\;\\mathcal{A}^{a}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\,L_{a}\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right],$  |\nL199: with $L_{a}$ the basis elements of $\\mathfrak{g}$ acting in the representation carried by $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$. The Tensor Field may be an ordinary vector, $\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\hat{v}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)$, or a contraction $\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\sum_{k}T^{\\alpha}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{k}}\\,\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{1}}\\!\\cdots\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{k}}$.\nL200: \nL201:   * •\nL202: \nL203: Action of an $L_{\\infty}$-algebra on the (graded) Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\nL204:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]:=\\;\\sum_{m}\\mathcal{A}^{a}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\,b_{m}\\!\\bigl(e_{a},\\,\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr),\\dots,\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\bigr),$  |\nL205: \nL206: where $b_{m}$ are the higher multilinear brackets and $e_{a}$ a basis. Again, $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ may be a graded Vector $\\hat{v}$ or a graded contracted Tensor as above.\n"
}
